In article <ho0mq1$8gp$1@news.urz.uni-heidelberg.de>, Dennis Grevenstein <dennis.grevenstein@gmail.com> writes:
> Hi,
> 
> I have an old DEC 3000/500 Alpha and some really old OSF/1 cds.
> I would like to get this machine back in its 1993 vintage original
> condition. I have various versions of OSF/1 like 1.2, 1.3 and 2.0.
> Any of them would be fine, I guess, but I would really prefer some
> 1.X release. One of the CDs is marked as a "1.2T-something" release
> from 1992. Is that some field test or prerelease software?

   I'm not sure about OSF/1, but generally a lettered variant is a
   version released soley to support some new hardware that is coming
   out before the next .x release.
 
> Anyway, problem is that I can't install any of those old versions.
> They boot and continue to the point where I can choose the harddisk
> to install the OS on and then they all crash with a kernel panic.
> Surprisingly, 3.2 and later release install and run just fine.

   If the OS release predates the hardware ship date, support for
   that hardware may simply not be included in that OS release.  The
   most important part likely to be missing would be support for the 
   I/O bus setup, so the kernel works up until you try to use some
   part of the system that the kernel didn't have yet.  This is more
   likely to be in the area of bus types and configurations than
   just the disk drives themselves.

> Aside from that, I'm really out of ideas. I have also tried 
> AXP VMS 1.0 and this one also crashes while OpenVMS 6.1 runs
> fine.

   I'm quite certain DEC 5000/300 Alphas came quite a bit later than 
   VMS 1.0 for Alpha.  There would be no support for them in 1.0.



