Had  problems this evening - in order to get Multinet installed on the
VAXstation 4000/60 that I run 24/7 I decided to backup the existing
2GB hard drive and install a pair of 18GB drives (intending to create
a shadowset). After I'd done the backup by booting off an external
drive in a BA356 and backing up to a different drive in the enclosure
I replaced the existing drive and rebooted the box. At that point I
got a nasty smell and some crackling sounds. Oh boy! I thought I'd
overloaded the power supply - the 18GB drives are 10K 80 pin server
drives with a 80->50 pin converters but admittedly they're probably no
more power hungry than older 50 pin SCSI drives. I took both drives
out and tried the power supply again - fizzing again.

I took the power supply out and opened it up to see if anything had
obviously blown. I'd noted that the power supply was complaining even
when switched off. Previously I'd plugged a power meter into a
VAXstation 4000/90 and noted that even when turned off the power
supply still drew 20 watts or so. The mains-rated switch at the front
of these boxes in reality connects up to a low power part of the
circuit - the HV transformer is permanently enabled when the box is
plugged in.

I couldn't find any obvious component damage - I almost gave up at
that point because to get the circuit board out of the cage I needed
to unsolder the connections on the power inlet and outlet connectors.
However, I persisted and upon visual inspection of the underside of
the PCB noted that the main transformer had one connector that was a
dry joint - it had worked free and was loose - hence the arcing.
Having reflowed all the joints on the transformer, brush dusted the
board, casing and fans, reassembled and resoldered the wires to the
power connectors I can happily report that the power supply is now
fully operational again. In the end having powered up with the two 10K
drives I decided they were causing too much vibration (making a really
irritating low frequency hum even at 10 metres) so I've put in a 9GB
50 pin drive which although making some weird clicking noises I'm
going to go with (I think the head moving mechanism is noisy as
opposed to the kind of terminal clicking noises I have previously
encountered).

So, the new internal drive is now being restored (I even found the
appropriate sledge to screw the drive to) from a canister in the BA356
and then hopefully life will calm down a bit for a while!

Regards, Mark.


