Well, it would be a "kit" of sorts (among other things, it's unusual
to find generic FPGA cards with DEC-style card edges and line drivers
on 'em), but I should note that it takes a long time for software
people to learn how to effectively learn how to program FPGAs (it's
not actually that similar to software; Verilog and VHDL are called
"Hardware Description Languages" because they're really closer to
describing a schematic with text than writing a program).  That said,
if I were building it, it would certainly be aimed towards the
hobbyist market, so it would use parts that can be built with only the
free FPGA tools and the FPGA architecture examples would be using open-
source cores (like those available at OpenCores.org).

But yes, it would be a straight-up QBUS device that would work
straight away.  If I included some RAM, one could actually even use it
as a full PDP-11 CPU (though making one of those would take quite a
lot of effort).  There's a lot you can do with a "small" FPGA.

Anyone interested?


