> cycles, etc.....otherwise, whats the point?)
> IIRC, DS8838 =A0and 74ls244s =A0work nicely as bus interfaces, and were
> available afaik, about a year ago. What drivers did you find? =A0Sounds
> like you might be further along than I am, but willing to help out.
> Am interested in both qbus and unibus versions.
> -John

The main problem with the 74LS244 (or 245, which is what you'd likely
want to use for the bidirectional pins) is that it's not open-
collector.  This could cause potentially catastrophic damage (though
in practice, not usually) if a badly-behaved device were to be driving
high while another device is pulling low.  The DS8838 is almost ideal
for DEC bus applications (indeed, it almost looks like it was meant
for it), but it is sadly obsolete since about 2002.

The drivers I found that came the closest were the 74AS641/642 (642 is
inverting, but almost impossible to find).  They're basically open-
collector '245s which can take 64mA constant sink current.  Some work
will have to be done as far as matching impedance, certainly, but you
don't run the risk of blowing something up like with the '244/'245.
The '641 is available in quantity from Mouser and while it's not
inverting like typical DEC drivers, that doesn't matter so much on an
FPGA (just invert it before sending it out from or bringing it in to
the core logic, and you're good).

The way I see it, a single FPGA ought to be able to do the job,
especially if you put a soft-core processor in there (a lot of the
adaptors had processors on them anyway; the DELQA had a 68k on it that
was close to the power of the PDP-11 it talked to).  Alternatively,
you could get away with a much smaller FPGA or even a CPLD (mostly for
bus interface and DMA) if you bolted a real microcontroller onto it,
and it might save some money.  I'm a fan of Motorola's Coldfire
series, and there are also a few good cheap ARMs that would work well
for a reasonable cost.

As it stands, for small quantities (~10), the board I'm envisioning
will probably end up costing a bit over $200 (with assembly, the FPGA
and the bus drivers being the largest components).


